---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { site } from '../data/site-config';
import { SITES } from '../lib/sites';
import { breadcrumbJsonLd } from '../lib/seo';

const canonical = `${SITES.main}/compliance/`;
const jsonLd = [
  breadcrumbJsonLd([
    { name: 'Home', url: `${SITES.main}/` },
    { name: 'Compliance', url: canonical },
  ]),
];
---

<BaseLayout
  site={site}
  title="Compliance & Regulatory"
  description="Charcoal compliance and regulatory notes for EU, UK, USA — EUDR, emissions, packaging, labeling, food-contact claims and customs. Not legal advice."
  canonical={canonical}
  jsonLd={jsonLd}
  breadcrumbs={[{ name: 'Home', url: '/' }, { name: 'Compliance', url: '/compliance/' }]}
>
  <div class="page-title container">
    <h1>Compliance &amp; Regulatory</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Regulatory notes for importing charcoal into the EU, UK and USA. This page is informational —
      it is not legal advice, and it does not make absolute sales claims about compliance.
    </p>
  </div>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container prose">
      <div class="callout">
        <span class="callout-title">Not legal advice</span>
        <p>
          Compliance depends on the specific regulation, HS classification, import purpose and current
          official interpretation. Verify all requirements with your customs broker or a qualified
          advisor before importing.
        </p>
      </div>

      <h2>Common Import Framework: EU, UK and USA</h2>
      <p>
        Importing charcoal into most markets involves the same layers, regardless of destination:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Tariff classification</strong> — charcoal is typically classified under HS heading 4402 (wood charcoal, including shell or nut charcoal). The specific subheading determines duty and any documentary requirements.</li>
        <li><strong>Customs declaration</strong> — correct value, origin and classification on entry.</li>
        <li><strong>Dangerous-goods transport</strong> — charcoal ships as UN 1361, Class 4.2 (self-heating), which carries its own declaration and stowage rules before the goods even reach the border.</li>
        <li><strong>Product-specific requirements</strong> — varies by market, as outlined below.</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="updated">
        Requirements change frequently. The statements below reflect publicly documented areas as of
        2026-10-07 and are marked for periodic review; always re-verify against the current official text.
      </p>

      <h2>European Union</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>EU Deforestation Regulation (EUDR)</strong> — see the dedicated note below; scope depends on commodity classification and HS code.</li>
        <li><strong>REACH / chemical safety</strong> — applicable to substances and articles placed on the EU market; not automatically in or out of scope for a carbon product.</li>
        <li><strong>Packaging &amp; labeling</strong> — packaging waste and labeling rules vary by member state and product.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>United Kingdom</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>UK REACH</strong> — the UK operates its own chemicals regime post-Brexit.</li>
        <li><strong>UK Timber / deforestation</strong> — the UK's deforestation due-diligence approach is separate from EUDR; verify current requirements.</li>
        <li><strong>Customs &amp; tariff</strong> — UK Global Tariff applies; classification follows the UK's own tariff schedule.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>United States</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>HTS classification</strong> — US tariff treatment follows the Harmonized Tariff Schedule (heading 4402).</li>
        <li><strong>Section 301 duties</strong> — additional duties have applied to many Chinese-origin goods; rates change, so verify the current USTR status for the specific 10-digit code.</li>
        <li><strong>Food-contact / grilling claims</strong> — charcoal is generally not a food-contact material, but any grilling or food-related claim must be accurate.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>EUDR: Uncertainty, Not Exemption</h2>
      <p>
        The EU Deforestation Regulation (Regulation (EU) 2023/1115) covers seven commodities: cattle,
        cocoa, coffee, oil palm, rubber, soya and wood. Wood charcoal under HS heading 4402 appears in
        its product list. Coconut shell charcoal is commonly classified under a coconut-shell
        subheading rather than "wood", and coconut is not one of the seven listed commodities — which
        supports an argument that lots made solely from coconut shell fall outside the core scope.
      </p>
      <div class="callout-pending">
        <span class="callout-title">Do not treat this as a blanket exemption</span>
        <p>
          We do not state that "coconut charcoal is EUDR-exempt." Whether a consignment is in scope
          depends on the declared HS subheading, the import purpose and the current official
          interpretation, and heading 4402's inclusion in Annex I means operators may still be asked
          to demonstrate due diligence. Application dates have also shifted (postponed to 30 December
          2026 for large/medium operators and downstream traders, and 30 June 2027 for micro/small
          enterprises, per Regulation (EU) 2025/2650). Confirm your position with a customs
          compliance specialist.
        </p>
      </div>

      <h2>Areas to Review</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Emissions &amp; combustion</strong> — combustion products and indoor-air considerations may be subject to national rules.</li>
        <li><strong>Food-contact / grilling claims</strong> — any food-related claim must be accurate and compliant.</li>
        <li><strong>Packaging &amp; labeling</strong> — packaging waste, labeling and language requirements vary by destination market.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Source &amp; Recency</h2>
      <p class="updated">
        These notes reflect general regulatory areas as of 2026-10-07 and are marked for periodic review.
        Specific requirements are confirmed per order and destination market with the buyer's broker.
        This page is informational and does not constitute legal advice.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
